EVENTS = [
    dict(id="draining-the-forum", periodId="kingdom-600bce", date="c. 600 BCE", kind="construction", at=dict(spotId="cloaca-maxima"), effect="construction", title="The Cloaca Maxima drains the Forum",
         text="Under the Tarquin kings the marshy valley between the Palatine and Capitoline was drained by a great channel, the Cloaca Maxima, running down to the Tiber. Livy says the kings set the common people to the work, which was hated for its hardship. Probably an open ditch at first, it was later vaulted in stone. Without it the Forum could never have become the city's centre.",
         sourceIds=["livy", "wiki-cloaca", "claridge"]),
    dict(id="capitoline-foundations", periodId="kingdom-600bce", date="c. 580–509 BCE", kind="construction", at=dict(landmarkId="temple-of-jupiter"), effect="construction", title="Building Jupiter's temple on the Capitol",
         text="Tradition credits the Tarquins with levelling the Capitoline summit and raising the huge Temple of Jupiter Optimus Maximus, dedicated in 509 BCE, the first year of the Republic. Its podium, about 62 by 54 metres, is the largest known Etruscan-style temple base. Livy tells how workers digging the foundations found a human head, read as an omen that Rome would be head of the world.",
         sourceIds=["livy", "wiki-temple-jupiter", "claridge"]),
    dict(id="circus-laid-out", periodId="kingdom-600bce", date="traditionally 6th century BCE", kind="festival", at=dict(landmarkId="circus-maximus"), effect="crowd", title="First games in the Murcia valley",
         text="Livy places the first Circus games under Tarquinius Priscus, who marked out the valley between Palatine and Aventine and let senators and knights build their own wooden stands. Nothing permanent existed yet: the floor flooded, the turning posts were probably wooden, and most spectators stood on the slopes. Horse races for the god Consus were already held here, according to the legend of the Sabine women.",
         sourceIds=["livy", "wiki-circus-maximus"]),
    dict(id="scipio-triumph", periodId="republic-200bce", date="201 BCE", kind="procession", at=dict(landmarkId="roman-forum"), effect="procession", title="Scipio Africanus triumphs over Carthage",
         text="Returning from Zama, Scipio rode through the Forum and up to the Capitol in the most splendid triumph Rome had yet seen. Livy reports 123,000 pounds of silver carried in the procession and 400 asses paid to each soldier. The victory ended the Second Punic War; Livy notes he was the first general to be known by the name of a people he had conquered: Africanus.",
         sourceIds=["livy", "beard-triumph", "wiki-triumph"]),
    dict(id="basilica-porcia", periodId="republic-200bce", date="184 BCE", kind="construction", at=dict(landmarkId="roman-forum"), effect="construction", title="Cato builds Rome's first basilica",
         text="As censor in 184 BCE, Cato the Elder bought houses and shops beside the Curia and built the Basilica Porcia, the first of Rome's great roofed halls for business and law. Plutarch says Cato built it in the teeth of opposition. The building, Greek in form, housed the tribunes' bench; it burned in 52 BCE together with the Curia during the riot at Clodius's funeral and was never rebuilt.",
         sourceIds=["livy", "wiki-basilica-porcia", "claridge"]),
    dict(id="bacchanalia", periodId="republic-200bce", date="186 BCE", kind="civic", at=dict(spotId="comitium"), effect="crowd", title="The Senate suppresses the Bacchanalia",
         text="In 186 BCE the consul Postumius addressed a public meeting in the Forum denouncing secret night-time rites of Bacchus as a conspiracy against the state. Livy says the gates were guarded, thousands were arrested across Italy and many executed. The Senate's decree, preserved on a bronze tablet found in Calabria, limited gatherings to five people without permission. A freedwoman informer, Hispala, was rewarded.",
         sourceIds=["livy", "wiki-bacchanalia"]),
    dict(id="aqua-marcia", periodId="republic-200bce", date="144–140 BCE", kind="construction", at=dict(latlon=(41.8900, 12.5080)), effect="construction", title="Praetor Marcius builds the Aqua Marcia",
         text="The praetor Quintus Marcius Rex was charged with repairing the old aqueducts and bringing in a new one, 91 kilometres from springs in the upper Anio valley. Its last stretch ran on long arcades, the first on such a scale at Rome, and it was the first to reach the Capitol, which the Sibylline Books had supposedly forbidden. Frontinus praised its cold, clear water. It cost 180 million sesterces.",
         sourceIds=["frontinus", "wiki-aqua-marcia"]),
    dict(id="clodius-riot", periodId="republic-50bce", date="January 52 BCE", kind="riot", at=dict(landmarkId="curia"), effect="fire", title="Clodius's funeral pyre burns the Curia",
         text="After the gang leader Publius Clodius was killed on the Via Appia by Milo's men, his followers carried the corpse into the Forum. Urged on by two tribunes, the crowd built a pyre inside the Senate house from benches, tribunals and clerks' records. The Curia Hostilia burned to the ground with the Basilica Porcia beside it. Pompey was made sole consul to restore order; Cicero defended Milo and lost.",
         sourceIds=["asconius", "wiki-clodius"]),
    dict(id="pompey-theatre-opening", periodId="republic-50bce", date="55 BCE", kind="festival", at=dict(landmarkId="theatre-of-pompey"), effect="crowd", title="Pompey opens Rome's first stone theatre",
         text="Pompey dedicated his theatre in 55 BCE, during his second consulship, with games that Cicero, writing to a friend, found vulgar: six hundred mules in a tragedy, and elephants (twenty, says Pliny) slaughtered in the games, whose cries roused the crowd's pity rather than pleasure. A temple of Venus Victrix crowned the seating, letting Pompey call the whole theatre a temple and dodge the ban on permanent theatres.",
         sourceIds=["cicero-letters", "pliny", "wiki-theatre-pompey"]),
    dict(id="caesar-buys-land", periodId="republic-50bce", date="54 BCE", kind="construction", at=dict(landmarkId="forum-of-caesar"), effect="construction", title="Caesar buys land for a new forum",
         text="Cicero wrote to Atticus in 54 BCE that he and Caesar's agents had already spent sixty million sesterces buying private houses north of the old Forum to extend it. The result became the Forum of Caesar, dedicated unfinished in 46 BCE with a temple to Venus Genetrix, Caesar's claimed ancestress. It set the model every emperor followed: a forum bearing the builder's name, paid for with war booty from Gaul.",
         sourceIds=["cicero-letters", "wiki-forum-caesar", "claridge"]),
    dict(id="cicero-catiline", periodId="republic-50bce", date="8 November 63 BCE", kind="civic", at=dict(landmarkId="roman-forum"), effect="crowd", title="Cicero denounces Catiline in the Senate",
         text="On 8 November 63 BCE Cicero convened the Senate in the Temple of Jupiter Stator, at the foot of the Palatine, and delivered his First Catilinarian: \"How long, Catiline, will you abuse our patience?\" Senators moved away from Catiline's bench, leaving him alone. He left Rome that night. A month later five conspirators were strangled in the Tullianum beside the Forum, and Cicero told the crowd: \"They have lived.\"",
         sourceIds=["cicero-catilinarians", "sallust", "wiki-catiline"]),
    dict(id="great-fire-64", periodId="empire-100ce", date="19 July 64 CE", kind="disaster", at=dict(landmarkId="circus-maximus"), effect="fire", title="The Great Fire starts at the Circus",
         text="On the night of 18–19 July 64 CE fire broke out among the shops along the Circus Maximus, where flammable goods were stored, and raced through the valley driven by wind. Tacitus says it burned six days, then flared again; of fourteen regions only four escaped. Nero was at Antium when it began. He opened the Campus Martius to the homeless, then built his Golden House on the ashes.",
         sourceIds=["tacitus", "wiki-great-fire"]),
    dict(id="trajan-forum-works", periodId="empire-100ce", date="107–112 CE", kind="construction", at=dict(landmarkId="forum-of-trajan"), effect="construction", title="Cutting the hill for Trajan's Forum",
         text="Spoils from the Dacian Wars paid for the largest imperial forum. Apollodorus of Damascus's workmen cut away a spur joining the Quirinal to the Capitoline; the inscription on Trajan's Column says its height, about 35 metres with the base, shows how much hill was removed. The complex, dedicated in 112, held a basilica, two libraries and a great colonnaded square with an equestrian statue of Trajan at the centre.",
         sourceIds=["wiki-trajan-forum", "claridge", "cassius-dio"]),
    dict(id="colosseum-games-day", periodId="empire-100ce", date="a games day, c. 100 CE", kind="festival", at=dict(landmarkId="colosseum"), effect="crowd", title="A day at the amphitheatre",
         text="A full programme began with wild beast hunts in the morning, executions of criminals at midday, and gladiators in the afternoon. Fifty thousand spectators sat by rank, senators at the front, women and the poor at the top beneath the awnings worked by sailors. Martial, who saw the opening games in 80 CE, describes a rhinoceros tossing a bull. Seneca found the midday executions dismal.",
         sourceIds=["martial", "seneca", "wiki-colosseum"]),
    dict(id="martial-on-sale", periodId="empire-100ce", date="c. 98 CE", kind="daily", at=dict(spotId="argiletum-booksellers"), effect="none", title="Buying Martial's epigrams in the Argiletum",
         text="Martial tells readers to seek his books in the Argiletum, opposite Caesar's Forum, where the bookseller Atrectus's shop has doorposts covered with advertisements for poets; a copy smoothed with pumice and dressed in purple costs five denarii. Scribes copy by hand in the back room. Every day customers browse, authors loiter hoping to be recognised, and the smell of papyrus and cedar oil drifts into the street.",
         sourceIds=["martial", "wiki-argiletum"]),
    dict(id="tiber-flood-69", periodId="empire-100ce", date="69 CE", kind="disaster", at=dict(spotId="tiber-boatmen"), effect="none", title="The Tiber floods the lower city",
         text="In the spring of 69 CE, as Otho marched out against Vitellius, the Tiber rose over its banks and swamped the Campus Martius and the low ground of the Forum valley. Tacitus records collapsed tenements, people drowned in their shops and beds, food shortages, and the Via Flaminia, the army's route north, blocked by water. Romans read it as an omen.",
         sourceIds=["tacitus", "aldrete", "wiki-tiber-floods"]),
    dict(id="caracalla-baths-open", periodId="empire-215ce", date="216 CE", kind="festival", at=dict(landmarkId="baths-of-caracalla"), effect="crowd", title="The Baths of Caracalla open",
         text="Caracalla's enormous bathing complex south of the Aventine opened in 216, its main block alone covering over two hectares with pools, hot rooms, gymnasia and gardens for perhaps 1,600 bathers at once. A new branch of the Aqua Marcia, the Aqua Antoniniana, fed it. Entry was cheap or free, and crowds from the whole city came to see the vaulted halls, mosaics and colossal statues like the Farnese Hercules.",
         sourceIds=["wiki-baths-caracalla", "claridge", "historia-augusta"]),
    dict(id="geta-murdered", periodId="empire-215ce", date="December 211 CE", kind="civic", at=dict(landmarkId="palatine-palace"), effect="none", title="Caracalla murders his brother Geta",
         text="The two sons of Septimius Severus ruled jointly but hated each other, dividing the Palatine palace with separate entrances. In December 211 Caracalla arranged a reconciliation meeting in their mother Julia Domna's apartments and had centurions cut Geta down in her arms. Cassius Dio says Caracalla then had Geta's name chiselled from every monument and killed some 20,000 of his supporters; the erasure is still visible on the Severan Arch.",
         sourceIds=["cassius-dio", "herodian", "wiki-geta"]),
    dict(id="severan-arch-dedicated", periodId="empire-215ce", date="203 CE", kind="ceremony", at=dict(landmarkId="arch-of-septimius-severus"), effect="procession", title="The Senate dedicates Severus's arch",
         text="In 203, marking ten years of his reign, the Senate dedicated a triple arch at the north-west corner of the Forum to Septimius Severus and his sons Caracalla and Geta, celebrating victories over the Parthians. The reliefs show sieges of Mesopotamian cities and chained captives. The inscription, once in bronze letters, named Geta; after 211 his line was recut to praise Caracalla instead, the old letter-holes still showing.",
         sourceIds=["wiki-arch-severus", "claridge"]),
    dict(id="forma-urbis", periodId="empire-215ce", date="c. 203–211 CE", kind="civic", at=dict(latlon=(41.8930, 12.4878)), effect="none", title="A marble map of the whole city",
         text="Between 203 and 211 the Severan government fixed a marble plan of Rome, about 18 by 13 metres, to a wall of the Temple of Peace, a wall that now belongs to the church of Santi Cosma e Damiano. At a scale of about 1:240 it shows every street, tenement, shop and staircase in ground plan. About a tenth survives, in some 1,186 fragments; scholars are still fitting them together.",
         sourceIds=["wiki-forma-urbis", "claridge"]),
    dict(id="honorius-walls", periodId="late-400ce", date="401–403 CE", kind="construction", at=dict(landmarkId="porta-appia"), effect="construction", title="Honorius strengthens the Aurelian Walls",
         text="With Alaric's Goths in Italy, the emperor Honorius and his general Stilicho ordered the Aurelian Walls restored and raised between 401 and 403. The curtain was heightened to roughly double, gates like the Porta Appia got new towers and portcullises, and inscriptions on several gates named the emperors Arcadius and Honorius. Builders used brick and whatever stone was at hand. The city soon needed them.",
         sourceIds=["wiki-aurelian-walls", "claridge"]),
    dict(id="altar-of-victory", periodId="late-400ce", date="384 CE", kind="civic", at=dict(landmarkId="curia"), effect="none", title="Symmachus pleads for the Altar of Victory",
         text="In 384 Symmachus, prefect of Rome and voice of the pagan senators, begged the young Valentinian II to restore the Altar of Victory, removed from the Senate house in 382. \"We ask peace for the gods of our fathers,\" he wrote; \"not by one road alone can we reach so great a mystery.\" Ambrose answered and won. Save a brief return under the usurper Eugenius (392–394), the altar was never restored.",
         sourceIds=["symmachus", "ambrose", "wiki-altar-victory"]),
    dict(id="alaric-sack", periodId="late-400ce", date="24 August 410 CE", kind="disaster", at=dict(latlon=(41.9098, 12.4968)), effect="fire", title="Alaric's Goths enter the Porta Salaria",
         text="After three sieges, on 24 August 410 the Visigoths entered Rome through the Porta Salaria, probably opened from inside. For three days they looted houses and palaces; fires burned around the gate and the Gardens of Sallust. Alaric spared the churches of St Peter and St Paul as refuges. The first capture of Rome by a foreign enemy in 800 years shocked the empire; Jerome wrote that his voice failed.",
         sourceIds=["wiki-sack-410", "jerome"]),
    dict(id="pagan-rites-banned", periodId="late-400ce", date="391 CE", kind="civic", at=dict(landmarkId="temple-of-jupiter"), effect="none", title="Theodosius closes the temples",
         text="Laws of Theodosius in 391–392 forbade all sacrifice: no one might enter the temples or revere the images, and a later law banned even incense to the household gods. The great Temple of Jupiter on the Capitol, Rome's state sanctuary for 900 years, lost its priests and offerings. The Vandals stripped its gilded roof tiles in 455. Senators who had led the old religion quietly joined the churches.",
         sourceIds=["codex-theodosianus", "wiki-theodosius-pagans", "wiki-temple-jupiter"]),
    dict(id="jubilee-1300", periodId="medieval-1300", date="1300", kind="festival", at=dict(landmarkId="ponte-sant-angelo"), effect="crowd", title="Boniface VIII proclaims the first Jubilee",
         text="On 22 February 1300 Boniface VIII granted full forgiveness of sins to pilgrims who visited St Peter's and St Paul's on fifteen days during the year. The chronicler Giovanni Villani, who went, reckoned 200,000 foreigners in Rome at any moment and praised the city's handling of the crowds; Dante, in Inferno, describes the two-way lanes imposed on the Ponte Sant'Angelo. Innkeepers and money-changers grew rich.",
         sourceIds=["villani", "dante", "wiki-jubilee"]),
    dict(id="giotto-navicella", periodId="medieval-1300", date="c. 1300", kind="daily", at=dict(landmarkId="st-peters"), effect="none", title="Giotto's mosaic greets the pilgrims",
         text="Around 1300 (proposed dates run from about 1298 to 1310) Cardinal Jacopo Stefaneschi commissioned Giotto to make a vast mosaic of the Navicella, Christ walking on the water towards the apostles' storm-tossed boat, for the atrium of Old St Peter's. Every pilgrim crossing the courtyard passed beneath it: the ship was the Church, its pilot Peter. Only a heavily restored fragment survives in the new basilica's portico.",
         sourceIds=["wiki-navicella", "krautheimer"]),
    dict(id="frangipane-fortify", periodId="medieval-1300", date="12th–13th century", kind="construction", at=dict(landmarkId="colosseum"), effect="construction", title="The Frangipane turn the Colosseum into a fortress",
         text="In the 12th century the Frangipane clan, who dominated the Forum valley, made the Colosseum a stronghold, walling up arches, raising towers on the vaults and controlling the roads to the Lateran. Houses, workshops and a chapel filled the arcades; the Annibaldi held it after them until Henry VII gave it to the Senate and people of Rome in 1312. Earthquakes and quarrying did the rest.",
         sourceIds=["wiki-colosseum", "krautheimer"]),
    dict(id="cola-di-rienzo", periodId="medieval-1300", date="20 May 1347", kind="riot", at=dict(landmarkId="campidoglio"), effect="crowd", title="Cola di Rienzo seizes the Capitol",
         text="On Pentecost 1347 the notary Cola di Rienzo, after Mass at Sant'Angelo in Pescheria, marched in armour to the Capitol with a crowd and had new laws read out from the palace steps. The baronial senators fled; Cola took the title of Tribune and promised a reborn Roman republic. Petrarch cheered him. By December he had fled; in 1354 a mob killed him on the same hill.",
         sourceIds=["anonimo-romano", "wiki-cola"]),
    dict(id="marble-into-lime", periodId="medieval-1300", date="every working day", kind="daily", at=dict(spotId="calcararia"), effect="fire", title="Marble burns in the lime-kilns",
         text="Every working day the calcararii feed their kilns with broken statues, inscriptions and column drums from the ancient city. Burned for days, marble becomes lime for mortar. The quarter's name, Calcarario, survived in the church of Santa Lucia de Calcarario near Largo Argentina. Now and then someone copied an inscription before it burned, which is how some texts are known at all.",
         sourceIds=["lanciani", "krautheimer"]),
    dict(id="michelangelo-dome", periodId="renaissance-1560", date="1560", kind="construction", at=dict(landmarkId="st-peters"), effect="construction", title="Michelangelo at work on St Peter's",
         text="At 85, Michelangelo, chief architect since 1547 and refusing any salary, was raising the drum of the great dome, having stripped Sangallo's over-elaborate plan back to a compact Greek cross. In 1560 Pius IV confirmed him in office and the drum's paired columns were going up. He had a large wooden model of the dome made in 1558–61 so work could follow his design after his death in 1564.",
         sourceIds=["vasari", "ackerman", "wiki-st-peters"]),
    dict(id="ghetto-decree", periodId="renaissance-1560", date="14 July 1555", kind="civic", at=dict(spotId="ghetto"), effect="none", title="Paul IV walls in the Jews",
         text="With the bull Cum nimis absurdum of July 1555, Paul IV ordered Rome's Jews, long settled in Trastevere and the Sant'Angelo quarter, into a walled enclosure beside the Tiber, locked at night. They had to wear yellow hats, sell their property, keep one synagogue and trade only in rags and second-hand goods. Its opening words, calling it absurd for Jews to live among Christians, set the pattern for three centuries.",
         sourceIds=["wiki-cum-nimis", "wiki-roman-ghetto", "stinger"]),
    dict(id="campidoglio-works", periodId="renaissance-1560", date="from 1538", kind="construction", at=dict(landmarkId="campidoglio"), effect="construction", title="Michelangelo redesigns the Capitol",
         text="After Charles V's visit in 1536, Paul III moved the bronze Marcus Aurelius there in 1538 and asked Michelangelo to redesign it. He turned the square towards St Peter's, gave the Senators' Palace a double stair and planned twin palaces framing a trapezoid with an oval pavement. Work was slow: in 1560 only the Senators' stair was done; the Conservators' Palace followed from 1563 and the pavement waited until 1940.",
         sourceIds=["wiki-campidoglio", "ackerman"]),
    dict(id="pasquinades", periodId="renaissance-1560", date="most mornings", kind="daily", at=dict(spotId="pasquino"), effect="crowd", title="Pasquino speaks again",
         text="Most mornings since about 1501 Romans have found verses pasted to the battered ancient statue at the corner of the Orsini palace (now Palazzo Braschi), mocking cardinals, popes and prices in Latin or Roman dialect. Adrian VI reportedly wanted the statue thrown in the Tiber; under Paul IV the pasquinades were savage. Crowds read and copy them before the police tear them down. Pasquino has rivals: Marforio, Madama Lucrezia.",
         sourceIds=["wiki-pasquino", "hibbert", "partner"]),
    dict(id="tiber-flood-1557", periodId="renaissance-1560", date="15 September 1557", kind="disaster", at=dict(spotId="tiber-mills"), effect="none", title="The great Tiber flood of 1557",
         text="On 15 September 1557 the Tiber rose to nearly 19 metres on the Ripetta gauge, close to the record flood of 1530, drowning the Campo Marzio, the Ghetto and Trastevere. The floating mills moored by the Tiber Island broke loose and smashed against the bridges; bread ran short for days. Mud lay in the churches; a plaque at Santa Maria sopra Minerva marks the level. Embankments came only after 1870.",
         sourceIds=["wiki-tiber-floods", "hibbert", "aldrete"]),
]

# One description each (≤ 45 words): what kind of place, who lived/worked there, how it felt. `periods` is fixed.
DISTRICTS = [
    dict(id="subura", name="Subura", periods=ANCIENT,
         description="The crowded valley between the Esquiline, Viminal and Quirinal, reached via the Argiletum. Tall tenements, shops, taverns and brothels; Martial calls it loud; Juvenal would prefer a barren island. Caesar grew up here. Augustus's firewall shut it off from the marble centre."),
    dict(id="campus-martius", name="Campus Martius", periods=["kingdom-600bce"] + ANCIENT,
         description="The floodplain north-west of the old city, where the army drilled and citizens voted. Mostly open fields under the kings and early Republic; Pompey, Agrippa and the emperors filled it with theatres, baths, porticoes and the Pantheon. By 400 CE it was Rome's monumental showpiece."),
    dict(id="velabrum", name="Velabrum", periods=["kingdom-600bce"] + ANCIENT,
         description="Low ground between the Palatine and Capitoline, the old marsh drained by the Cloaca Maxima. A busy commercial quarter of oil, wine, perfume and food dealers; Plautus and Horace mention its shops. The Vicus Tuscus crossed it from the Forum to the cattle market."),
    dict(id="forum-boarium", name="Forum Boarium", periods=["kingdom-600bce"] + ANCIENT,
         description="The cattle market beside the Tiber's first landing place, Rome's oldest trading quarter and site of the Ara Maxima of Hercules. Temples round and rectangular, the river port and warehouses lined it; under the Empire the money-changers' guild raised the Arch of the Argentarii here."),
    dict(id="aventine", name="Aventine", periods=ANCIENT,
         description="The plebeian hill south of the Circus, outside the sacred boundary until Claudius. Temples of Ceres, Diana and Mercury guarded the commoners' interests; granaries and the port lay below. Under the Empire rich houses climbed it; the baths of Sura and Decius stood on top."),
    dict(id="trastevere", name="Trans Tiberim (Trastevere)", periods=ANCIENT + LATER,
         description="Across the river: a working quarter of boatmen, tanners, potters and millers, many of them immigrants, Jews and Syrians, beside Caesar's gardens and the naval barracks. In the Middle Ages a dense, proudly separate neighbourhood around Santa Maria in Trastevere, inside the Aurelian Walls' loop."),
    dict(id="esquiline", name="Esquiline", periods=ANCIENT,
         description="Rome's largest hill: once the paupers' burial ground outside the Servian Wall, which Maecenas turned into gardens. Mansions and imperial parks spread over the top; crowded streets filled the slopes below, then Nero's Golden House and Trajan's Baths. Later Santa Maria Maggiore crowned it."),
    dict(id="caelian", name="Caelian", periods=ANCIENT,
         description="The long ridge south-east of the Colosseum: in the Republic ordinary houses and tufa quarries, under the Empire a quiet quarter of senators' mansions, the Castra Peregrina and the vast Temple of Claudius. Churches like Santi Giovanni e Paolo rose on their ruins."),
    dict(id="testaccio", name="Emporium district", periods=ANCIENT,
         description="The river port south of the Aventine, laid out from 193 BCE: the Emporium quay, the Porticus Aemilia and warehouses for grain, wine and oil. Dockers, clerks and mule drivers worked here. Discarded oil amphorae piled up into Monte Testaccio, a hill of sherds."),
    dict(id="borgo", name="Borgo (the Leonine City)", periods=LATER,
         description="The pilgrim suburb between Castel Sant'Angelo and St Peter's, walled by Leo IV in the 850s after a Saracen raid. Hostels of the Saxons, Frisians and Lombards, relic-sellers, money-changers and taverns lined its streets. By the 16th century the papal palace ruled it."),
    dict(id="abitato", name="The abitato (inhabited city)", periods=LATER,
         description="The crowded Tiber bend where medieval and Renaissance Romans actually lived: the old Campus Martius, with narrow lanes, baronial towers, markets at Campo de' Fiori and the Pantheon, and the bankers' street by the bridge. Floods, mud and cardinals' palaces side by side."),
    dict(id="disabitato", name="The disabitato", periods=LATER,
         description="The empty two-thirds inside the Aurelian Walls: vineyards, ruins, pasture, marsh and scattered churches and monasteries. The Forum was the Campo Vaccino, cow pasture; the Baths of Caracalla a quarry. Pilgrims crossed it on foot to reach the Lateran and San Lorenzo. Wolves were reported."),
    dict(id="ghetto-district", name="Ghetto", periods=["renaissance-1560"],
         description="The walled Jewish enclosure beside the Tiber, a few flood-prone hectares around the Portico d'Ottavia, locked nightly from 1555. Some 3,000 people crammed into houses that grew upwards; rag trade, tailoring and pawnbroking were the permitted trades. One building, the Cinque Scole, held five synagogues."),
    dict(id="palatine-district", name="Palatine", periods=["kingdom-600bce", "republic-200bce", "republic-50bce"],
         description="The hill of Rome's founding legend, where visitors were shown the hut of Romulus. In the Republic an aristocratic address: Cicero, Clodius, Hortensius and Catulus had houses here beside the temples of Magna Mater and Victoria. Augustus's house began its total takeover by palaces."),
    dict(id="capitoline-district", name="Capitoline", periods=ALL,
         description="A small, steep hill and the state's religious heart: the Temple of Jupiter Optimus Maximus on one summit, the Arx and Juno Moneta on the other, the Tabularium between. The medieval Senators' Palace sat on the Tabularium; Michelangelo's piazza remade it as civic Rome."),
]

# One blurb each (≤ 30 words): where it went, who used it, one telling detail.
ROADS = [
    dict(id="via-sacra", name="Via Sacra", blurb="The Forum's sacred way, down from the Velia past the Regia and the Temple of Castor towards the Capitol. Route of triumphs; lined with jewellers' and luxury shops."),
    dict(id="clivus-capitolinus", name="Clivus Capitolinus", blurb="The paved ramp winding up from the Temple of Saturn to the Temple of Jupiter, the last stretch of every triumph. Its basalt paving partly survives."),
    dict(id="argiletum", name="Argiletum", blurb="From the Forum north-east into the Subura, past the Curia: shoemakers and booksellers, including Martial's bookseller Atrectus. Domitian's Forum Transitorium later swallowed its first stretch."),
    dict(id="vicus-tuscus", name="Vicus Tuscus", blurb="The 'Etruscan street' from the Forum past the Temple of Castor to the Velabrum and Circus. Shops of silk, perfume and incense dealers; a reputation for shady characters."),
    dict(id="vicus-jugarius", name="Vicus Jugarius", blurb="Skirting the Capitoline's southern foot from the Forum to the Forum Boarium and river. Named for yoke-makers or an altar of Juno Juga; carters and traders used it."),
    dict(id="via-lata", name="Via Lata / Via Flaminia", blurb="The Via Flaminia's straight run through the Campus Martius to the Capitoline, 'the broad street'. Imperial arches spanned it; from the 1460s it was the Corso, the Carnival racecourse."),
    dict(id="via-appia", name="Via Appia", blurb="The queen of roads, begun 312 BCE by Appius Claudius to Capua, later Brindisi. Leaving by the Porta Capena between tombs; armies marched out, eastern goods came in."),
    dict(id="via-ostiensis", name="Via Ostiensis", blurb="Along the Tiber's left bank 25 kilometres to Ostia, the grain port. Carts and towed barges shared the route; St Paul was buried beside it at the second milestone."),
    dict(id="via-tiburtina", name="Via Tiburtina", blurb="East through the Esquiline gate 30 kilometres to Tivoli, whose travertine quarries supplied the Colosseum. Later pilgrims took it to San Lorenzo fuori le Mura."),
    dict(id="via-labicana", name="Via Labicana / Praenestina", blurb="Twin roads out of the Porta Praenestina south-east to Labici and Palestrina. Helena's mausoleum and catacombs lay on the Labicana; aqueduct arcades marched beside the Praenestina."),
    dict(id="via-nomentana", name="Via Nomentana", blurb="North-east 23 kilometres to Nomentum through the Porta Collina, later the Porta Nomentana. Sant'Agnese and Constantina's mausoleum stand at its second mile; Pius IV built the Porta Pia."),
    dict(id="via-salaria", name="Via Salaria", blurb="The ancient salt road north-east up the Tiber valley to the Sabine hills, older than Rome. The Gardens of Sallust lay beside it; Alaric's Goths entered its gate in 410."),
    dict(id="via-aurelia", name="Via Aurelia", blurb="West over the Tiber and the Janiculum to the Etruscan coast and Pisa, begun around 241 BCE. Travellers left through the Porta Aurelia, now the Porta San Pancrazio."),
    dict(id="via-triumphalis", name="Via Triumphalis", blurb="From the Vatican plain across the Tiber towards the Campus Martius, probably the route of returning generals before Augustus. Its line later carried pilgrims down from Monte Mario."),
    dict(id="alta-semita", name="Alta Semita", blurb="The 'high path' along the Quirinal ridge from the Capitoline towards the Porta Collina. Senators' houses and the Baths of Constantine lined it; Pius IV's Strada Pia followed its course."),
    dict(id="clivus-palatinus", name="Clivus Palatinus", blurb="The paved road climbing from the Arch of Titus onto the Palatine, main approach to the imperial palace. Basalt paving survives; petitioners and courtiers queued along it."),
    dict(id="via-papalis", name="Via Papalis", blurb="The papal procession route from St Peter's through the abitato, past Pasquino and the Capitol, to the Lateran: every new pope rode it to take possession of his cathedral."),
    dict(id="via-giulia-road", name="Via Giulia", blurb="Julius II's straight street along the Tiber, laid out by Bramante from 1508: a kilometre of palaces, bankers and the Florentines' church, Rome's first planned Renaissance avenue."),
    dict(id="via-del-pellegrino", name="Via del Pellegrino", blurb="The pilgrims' street from Campo de' Fiori to the bankers' quarter, part of the Via Papalis; goldsmiths and rosary-sellers packed it, and papal corteges squeezed through."),
    dict(id="via-dei-banchi", name="Via dei Banchi", blurb="The bankers' street at the bridgehead, where Florentine and Genoese houses, the Chigi bank and the papal mint handled Jubilee cash and Church revenues. Printers followed the money."),
    dict(id="via-recta", name="Via Recta (Via dei Coronari)", blurb="A straight ancient street across the Campus Martius from the bridgehead towards the Via Lata. Its medieval successor, Via dei Coronari, was named for the rosary-sellers serving pilgrims."),
    dict(id="corso", name="Via del Corso", blurb="The old Via Lata, renamed for the carnival races (corse) of riderless horses, run along it from Paul II's time in the 1460s. Palaces slowly replaced tenements."),
    dict(id="via-pia", name="Via Pia (Strada Pia)", blurb="Pius IV's new straight avenue of 1561 along the Quirinal ridge to Michelangelo's Porta Pia. Villas and vineyards lined it through the disabitato; it is now Via XX Settembre."),
]

# One blurb each (≤ 30 words).
HILLS = [
    dict(id="capitoline", name="Capitoline", blurb="Lowest but holiest of the seven hills: citadel, Temple of Jupiter and state archive. Goal of every triumph, seat of the medieval Senate; Michelangelo's piazza faces St Peter's."),
    dict(id="palatine", name="Palatine", blurb="Rome's birthplace, between Forum and Circus. Aristocrats' houses under the Republic became one continuous imperial palace; the word 'palace' comes from its name. Later vineyards and Farnese gardens."),
    dict(id="aventine-hill", name="Aventine", blurb="The plebeian hill across the Circus from the Palatine, long outside the sacred boundary. Temples of Ceres and Diana, granaries below; later rich villas, then Santa Sabina and Santa Prisca."),
    dict(id="caelian-hill", name="Caelian", blurb="Long ridge south-east of the Forum, said to be named for the Etruscan Caelius Vibenna. Imperial mansions and the Temple of Claudius; later churches among vineyards."),
    dict(id="esquiline-hill", name="Esquiline", blurb="Rome's biggest hill, with two spurs, the Oppian (and Fagutal) and Cispian. Once the paupers' cemetery, then Maecenas's gardens, Nero's Golden House, Trajan's Baths and Santa Maria Maggiore."),
    dict(id="viminal", name="Viminal", blurb="The smallest and least remembered hill, between Quirinal and Esquiline, named for its osier willows. Diocletian's Baths covered its north end; the Praetorian camp stood beyond."),
    dict(id="quirinal", name="Quirinal", blurb="The northern ridge, home of the Sabine god Quirinus, later senators' houses, the Temple of Serapis and the Baths of Constantine. Popes built their summer palace there after 1583."),
    dict(id="janiculum", name="Janiculum", blurb="The high ridge west of the Tiber, not one of the seven. Janus's hill guarded the Etruscan side; water-mills worked its slopes. San Pietro in Montorio marks Peter's supposed crucifixion."),
    dict(id="pincian", name="Pincian", blurb="The 'hill of gardens' north of the Campus Martius, with the villas of Lucullus, Sallust and the Pincii. Outside the Republican city, inside Aurelian's walls; later Trinità dei Monti."),
    dict(id="vatican-hill", name="Vatican Hill", blurb="Across the Tiber: an unhealthy slope with Nero's circus and cemeteries. Peter's tomb there became Constantine's basilica; by 1560 the papal palace and the half-built new St Peter's stood here."),
    dict(id="testaccio-hill", name="Monte Testaccio", blurb="An artificial hill about 35 metres high of broken olive-oil amphorae dumped from the Emporium warehouses, mostly in the 2nd and 3rd centuries: roughly 53 million jars."),
]

WATER = [
    dict(id="tiber", name="Tiber", blurb="Rome's river, yellow with silt, navigable by barge to the Emporium and later Ripa Grande and Ripetta. Its floods regularly drowned the low city; floating mills turned beside Tiber Island."),
]

# Stories: 4–6 steps each. Each step: dict(landmarkId=...) or dict(spotId=...), title (≤ 6 words), text (≤ 70 words), when (optional), sourceIds.
STORIES = [
    dict(id="triumph-71ce", title="The Triumph of Vespasian and Titus", periodId="empire-100ce",
         summary="Follow Vespasian and Titus through the city on the day in 71 CE when they celebrated the conquest of Judaea, as the eyewitness Josephus described it.",
         sourceIds=["josephus", "beard-triumph", "wiki-triumph"],
         steps=[dict(landmarkId="circus-maximus", title="The procession enters the city", when="71 CE",
                     text="The emperors spent the night outside the walls by the Temple of Isis and at dawn took their seats at the Portico of Octavia, where the Senate and the army cheered them. Josephus says the parade was routed through the theatres so more people could see it; its way probably ran round the Circus Maximus. Crowds pack stands and rooftops; wagons of spoils roll past.",
                     sourceIds=["josephus", "beard-triumph"]),
                dict(landmarkId="arch-of-titus", title="The spoils of the Temple",
                     text="Here, where Domitian later raised this arch to his dead brother, the most striking spoils went by: the golden table, the seven-branched lampstand and the scroll of the Jewish Law, carried shoulder-high. The arch's relief shows exactly this moment, soldiers in laurel wreaths bearing the menorah. Josephus also describes towering floats, three or four storeys high, showing cities burning and fleets sinking.",
                     sourceIds=["josephus", "wiki-triumph"]),
                dict(landmarkId="roman-forum", title="The prisoner dies in the Forum",
                     text="At the edge of the Forum the procession halted. By ancient custom the triumph waited while the chief captive, Simon bar Giora, who had led the defence of Jerusalem, was dragged with a noose round his neck to the Tullianum prison below the Capitol and executed. When the shout came that he was dead, the crowd cheered and the emperors moved on to sacrifice.",
                     sourceIds=["josephus", "beard-triumph"]),
                dict(landmarkId="temple-of-jupiter", title="Sacrifice on the Capitol",
                     text="Vespasian and Titus climbed the Clivus Capitolinus to the Temple of Jupiter and offered prayers and the customary sacrifices; then feasting began across the city. Josephus ends with the fate of the spoils: the golden vessels went into Vespasian's new Temple of Peace, while the Law and the purple curtains of the sanctuary were kept in the palace. Rome was celebrating a war that Josephus says cost a million lives.",
                     sourceIds=["josephus"])]),
    dict(id="day-in-the-subura", title="A Day in the Subura", periodId="empire-100ce",
         summary="An ordinary day around 100 CE in Rome's noisiest quarter, followed hour by hour through the complaints and jokes of Martial and Juvenal.",
         sourceIds=["martial", "juvenal", "carcopino"],
         steps=[dict(spotId="pistrinum", title="Bakers before dawn", when="before dawn",
                     text="Before first light the bakery's blindfolded mule is already turning the lava millstone, and the smell of bread climbs to the tenants above. Martial lists the baker's din among the things that keep a Roman from sleeping. Slaves knead dough at a long table; round loaves stamped with the baker's mark go into the domed oven. By the first hour the first batch is on the counter.",
                     sourceIds=["martial", "carcopino"]),
                dict(spotId="subura-insula", title="Waking in the tenement", when="first hour",
                     text="Up five flights, the poor tenant has a single room: no kitchen, no water, a chamber pot emptied, or thrown, into the street. Juvenal notes that the tenant under the roof tiles, where the doves nest, burns last but has no way out. Below, the ground-floor shops take down their shutters. Clients hurry out in togas to their patron's morning greeting, hoping for the handout.",
                     sourceIds=["juvenal", "martial"]),
                dict(spotId="subura-barber", title="At the barber's", when="second hour",
                     text="Second hour: men wait on stools for a shave. The barber works with an iron razor and water, no soap; Martial jokes about one so slow that the beard grows back on the first cheek before he finishes the second. Here you hear the quarter's gossip, the price of bread, who died in last night's fire.",
                     sourceIds=["martial", "carcopino"]),
                dict(spotId="ludus-litterarius", title="Lessons under the awning", when="morning",
                     text="The schoolmaster has set up under a shop's awning, cane in hand; Martial complains that his shouting wakes the street before cockcrow. Boys and a few girls chant letters, scratch copies onto wax tablets and recite their reckoning tables. Fees are tiny, paid monthly. The children of freedmen sit beside slaves' children sent to learn enough to keep accounts.",
                     sourceIds=["martial", "carcopino"]),
                dict(spotId="thermopolium", title="Lunch at the counter", when="sixth hour",
                     text="Sixth hour, midday: the quarter stops for a quick meal at the cookshop counter, where jars sunk in the masonry hold chickpeas, lentils, sausages and warm spiced wine. Juvenal sneers at the hot bars and the sweating cooks. With no kitchen upstairs, everyone eats here. Dice rattle at the back table, though gambling is illegal; the aedile seldom looks in.",
                     sourceIds=["juvenal", "martial"]),
                dict(spotId="balneum", title="The afternoon bath", when="eighth hour",
                     text="Eighth hour: the baths open to men. A quadrans, the smallest coin, buys entry to a small neighbourhood balneum. Undress, oil, a ball game in the yard, then warm room, hot room and the cold plunge. Seneca once lodged above a bath and catalogued its noise: weightlifters grunting, the hair-plucker's victims shrieking, the sausage-seller and cake-seller each with his cry.",
                     sourceIds=["seneca", "martial"]),
                dict(landmarkId="colosseum", title="Evening at the amphitheatre", when="evening",
                     text="Towards evening the day's spectators pour out of the Colosseum and back up the Argiletum into the Subura. Those who stayed saw the afternoon's gladiators under the awning. Tonight dinner, for the lucky client, is at the patron's table; for the rest, bread and a cup at the bar. After dark the carts come in, since wheeled traffic is banned by day, and Juvenal's sleepless night begins.",
                     sourceIds=["juvenal", "martial"])]),
    dict(id="jubilee-1300", title="The Jubilee of 1300", periodId="medieval-1300",
         summary="Walk with the pilgrims of 1300, the first Holy Year, from the Tiber bridge to St Peter's and across the half-empty city to the Lateran.",
         sourceIds=["villani", "dante", "krautheimer", "wiki-jubilee"],
         steps=[dict(landmarkId="ponte-sant-angelo", title="Crossing the bridge in lanes", when="1300",
                     text="The old Pons Aelius is the only bridge to St Peter's. The crowd is so dense that the Romans have divided the roadway with a barrier: pilgrims going to the basilica keep one side, facing the castle, those returning keep the other, facing the Monte Giordano. Dante, who saw it, compares the sinners of Malebolge to this two-way traffic. Beggars and money-changers work both lanes.",
                     sourceIds=["dante", "wiki-ponte-sant-angelo"]),
                dict(landmarkId="st-peters", title="Fifteen visits for forgiveness",
                     text="The bull of February demands fifteen days of visits from foreigners, thirty from Romans. Inside the old Constantinian basilica, with its forest of columns, pilgrims shuffle past the confessio where Peter lies, wash at the bronze pine-cone fountain in the atrium, and leave coins. A chronicler from Asti saw two clerics at St Paul's raking in the offerings day and night.",
                     sourceIds=["villani", "krautheimer"]),
                dict(spotId="pilgrim-hostels", title="Where the pilgrims sleep",
                     text="Back in the Borgo the hostels of the nations, Saxon, Frisian and Lombard, are full; many sleep in the porticoes and in the fields. Villani praises the Romans for keeping bread, wine, meat, fish and oats plentiful, though hay and lodging went dear. Relic-sellers hawk little painted copies of the Veronica, the holy veil shown in St Peter's, for pilgrims to carry home.",
                     sourceIds=["villani", "krautheimer"]),
                dict(spotId="calcararia", title="Through the lime-burners' lane",
                     text="Crossing the abitato, pilgrims pass the Calcarario, where kilns burn ancient marble into lime beneath the ruins of the Theatre of Pompey and the Circus Flaminius. Smoke, heat and the crack of splitting statues. The guidebook many carry, the Mirabilia Urbis Romae, points the way to the wonders of the ancients while workmen quietly turn them into mortar.",
                     sourceIds=["mirabilia", "lanciani", "krautheimer"]),
                dict(landmarkId="campidoglio", title="The Senators' hill",
                     text="The Capitol is now the seat of the city's Senator, in a fortified palace on the Tabularium's ruins; the great stair up to Santa Maria in Aracoeli is not yet built. From the hill pilgrims look south over the Campo Vaccino, the cow pasture that was the Forum, to the Colosseum, now a baronial fortress. The Mirabilia still calls this the golden Capitol, head of the world.",
                     sourceIds=["mirabilia", "krautheimer"]),
                dict(landmarkId="lateran", title="The pope's cathedral",
                     text="The walk ends at the Lateran, Rome's cathedral, where Boniface VIII has just built a loggia for blessings; a fresco attributed to Giotto shows him proclaiming the Jubilee from it. Pilgrims visit the Sancta Sanctorum chapel's relics and climb the holy stairs beside the palace. The indulgence is complete. Beyond the basilica, Rome is vineyards and ruins all the way to the walls.",
                     sourceIds=["krautheimer", "wiki-lateran", "wiki-jubilee"])]),
    dict(id="new-rome-1560", title="Building the New Rome", periodId="renaissance-1560",
         summary="Rome in 1560 under Pius IV: markets, palaces, straight new streets, and Michelangelo, aged 85, directing the dome of St Peter's and the Capitol.",
         sourceIds=["ackerman", "partner", "stinger", "vasari"],
         steps=[dict(spotId="campo-de-fiori", title="Market in the morning", when="1560",
                     text="Campo de' Fiori is the market: stalls of vegetables, fish and hay, horse-dealers, and inns round the square for pilgrims and papal couriers. Executions happen here too. Behind the houses the curve of Pompey's ancient theatre still shapes the lanes. Rome has grown back to perhaps 80,000 people since the Sack of 1527.",
                     sourceIds=["wiki-campo-de-fiori", "partner"]),
                dict(landmarkId="palazzo-farnese", title="The Farnese palace",
                     text="The largest private palace in Rome, begun by Sangallo for Cardinal Alessandro Farnese and finished, after he became Paul III, by Michelangelo, who added the great cornice and the window over the door. In 1560 Paul III's grandsons, Cardinals Alessandro and Ranuccio, keep building and filling it with antiquities from the Baths of Caracalla, the Farnese Bull and Hercules. The garden front is unfinished.",
                     sourceIds=["wiki-palazzo-farnese", "ackerman"]),
                dict(spotId="via-giulia", title="Bramante's straight street",
                     text="Via Giulia runs a kilometre straight along the river, laid out for Julius II around 1508 for law courts that were never built; their rusticated blocks, the 'sofas', still jut from the pavement. Florentine bankers and churchmen have built palaces here, and San Giovanni dei Fiorentini is rising at its end. Carriages, newly fashionable, find it one of the few streets they fit through.",
                     sourceIds=["wiki-via-giulia", "partner"]),
                dict(landmarkId="st-peters", title="The dome takes shape",
                     text="At St Peter's the old basilica's nave still stands, walled off from the new crossing, where Bramante's four great piers, thickened by Michelangelo, carry the rising drum. The architect, 85 and often too frail to ride out, sends instructions from his house and has a wooden model built so no one can alter his design. In 1560 Pius IV confirms his authority against critics among the works deputies.",
                     sourceIds=["vasari", "ackerman", "wiki-st-peters"]),
                dict(landmarkId="campidoglio", title="The Capitol remade",
                     text="On the Capitol the Marcus Aurelius sits on Michelangelo's base at the centre of a square still half built. The Senators' Palace has its double stair; the Conservators' Palace, with its giant pilasters, will be begun in 1563 and finished in the 1580s; the Dioscuri reach the balustrade in 1583. Senators and guilds meet here, in the shadow of the popes.",
                     sourceIds=["wiki-campidoglio", "ackerman"]),
                dict(spotId="ghetto", title="The walled quarter at dusk",
                     text="The walk ends at the Ghetto gates, ordered by Paul IV five years ago. Pius IV would soon ease a few of the rules and let Jews trade more freely, but the walls stayed and the gates were locked each evening. Rag-dealers pack up their stalls; the river smell rises in the low streets. The new Rome of straight streets and domes is also a city of enclosure.",
                     sourceIds=["wiki-roman-ghetto", "wiki-cum-nimis", "stinger"])]),
]
